{"name":"Dynamite Red","hex":"#dd3311","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"dynamite","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A class of explosives made from nitroglycerine in an absorbent medium such as kieselguhr, used in mining and blasting.","A stick of trinitrotoluene (TNT)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To blow up with dynamite or other high explosive.","To dismantle or destroy."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dynamite"},{"term":"red","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of the setting sun, blood, and strawberries; the color which is evoked by the longest visible wavelengths (between about 625–740 nm), and a primary additive color.","A revolutionary socialist or (most commonly) a Communist;  a Bolshevik, a supporter of the Bolsheviks in the Russian Civil War."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a red hue.","Having an orange-brown or orange-blond colour; ginger."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/red"}],"entries":[{"title":"Dynamite","blocks":[{"text":"Dynamite is an explosive made of nitroglycerin, sorbents, and stabilizers. It was invented by Swedish chemist Alfred Nobel in 1866 and patented the following year."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Inserting_dynamite_into_hole.jpg/330px-Inserting_dynamite_into_hole.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dynamite"},{"title":"Red","blocks":[{"text":"Red is the color at the long wavelength end of the visible spectrum of light, next to orange and opposite violet. It has a dominant wavelength of approximately 625–750 nanometers. It is a primary color in the RGB color model and a secondary color in the CMYK color model, and is the complementary color of cyan. Reds range from the brilliant yellow-tinged scarlet and vermillion to bluish-red crimson, and vary in shade from the pale red pink to the dark red burgundy."}],"image":"h